<p>Fighting between Pakistan and Afghanistan’s Taliban-run authorities intensified over the weekend. Explosions and anti-aircraft fire were reported in Kabul, alongside claims of Pakistani aircraft activity over the capital. Afghan officials said they thwarted a Pakistani attempt to strike Bagram air base, the former US military facility north of Kabul, as reporting described a wider campaign targeting multiple Afghan military sites. Pakistan says it is responding to militant attacks and accuses Afghanistan of sheltering groups that strike inside Pakistan. Taliban-run authorities reject Pakistan’s account of the escalation. Both sides have issued sharply different casualty claims that the reporting could not independently verify. China said it is working on mediation, and international calls for a ceasefire have been reported but not yet heeded.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/G03IOKW09lZDDtJv_6ckxeOjlNEnHxa6dkqYGbfrk60JQ3iIPGpEY2TsD9ZbBf568TQf7MSf-lJ9I28eiMviA3pTOWlJoyW7tx1Pnod8th32SU6PWYE1w11HzqS9XNiEHf04JexdOL5NBoH2PNXaOeGKO_QfeKyExUpw1yjzaMdxQ2IYK2v3QlnCDkakdPBHIfAImeDwezAR5nR6' alt='Taliban fighters at the Bagram air field in Afghanistan in 2021.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Casualty claims: Pakistan’s information minister said at least 415 Taliban fighters and allied militants were killed and more than 580 wounded since Thursday. Afghanistan reported 78 dead over three days, including civilians and soldiers. Neither set of figures was independently verified.</li><li>Border flashpoints: Afghanistan reported deaths across several provinces — including Paktika, Khost, Kunar, Nangarhar and Kandahar — as clashes spread beyond a single crossing point.</li><li>Operations scope: Pakistan’s officials said strikes hit 46 locations and destroyed 182 Afghan checkpoints, along with armored vehicles and artillery. The Hindu described the operation as still continuing.</li><li>Regional stakes: Semafor said the confrontation adds to broader regional volatility after US and Israeli strikes on Iran, with the US, China and India all watching how the Pakistan-Afghanistan crisis develops.</li><li>Diplomatic disruption: The Hindu reported Pakistan’s Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif postponed a planned trip to Russia as the fighting escalated.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pakistan government: Pakistani officials say the strikes are a response to militancy they link to sanctuaries in Afghanistan. Semafor reported analysts saw room for further escalation — and “no clear off-ramp” — after blasts and gunfire in Kabul. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/01/2026/afghanistan-pakistan-conflict-intensifies'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Afghanistan’s authorities: Afghan officials said they thwarted a Pakistani attempt to strike Bagram air base, and described the flare-up as part of an intensifying campaign that has targeted dozens of military sites across the country. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/02/world/asia/pakistan-afghanistan-bagram-attack.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Taliban statements (as reported): The Taliban said they engaged Pakistani jets over Kabul as hostilities continued to escalate. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/afghan-taliban-reportedly-target-pakistani-jets-over-kabul/a-76178230'>DW</a>)</li><li>China: Beijing said it is working on mediation. Semafor noted China’s leverage through major investments tied to Pakistan, alongside its growing ties to the Taliban-run government in Kabul. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/01/2026/afghanistan-pakistan-conflict-intensifies'>Semafor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/03/01/2026/afghanistan-pakistan-conflict-intensifies'>Fighting intensifies between Afghanistan and Pakistan</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/afghan-taliban-reportedly-target-pakistani-jets-over-kabul/a-76178230'>Afghan Taliban reportedly target Pakistani jets over Kabul</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https:/